          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "**What adherence means:** In diabetes, adherence means carrying out the treatment plan between medical visits. The guidance describes three parts: taking glucose-lowering medication as prescribed, following the agreed diet and physical activity plan, and doing the glucose monitoring that has been agreed. Because diabetes is largely self-managed, most of the decisions that affect outcomes are made by the person day to day. (Source: *Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review*.)\n\n**Why it matters:** The general reference explains that sustained high blood glucose damages small blood vessels, which can affect the eyes, kidneys and nerves, including the feet. It also damages large blood vessels, which is linked to heart attack, stroke and poor circulation in the legs. Glucose control is one part of reducing these risks, alongside blood pressure, cholesterol and smoking. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*.) The glycemic target guidance describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many adults, mainly because it reduces small-vessel complications over time. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*.) Missed medication or plan changes can make reaching those targets harder.\n\n**Why people fall short:** The adherence guidance says that missing doses is usually not a matter of character. Common causes include cost, complicated schedules, side effects, low health literacy, depression and simple forgetting. Each has a different fix, so it helps to name the specific barrier. Side effects such as stomach upset are a common reason people quietly stop a medication, and guidance suggests raising them with a clinician rather than stopping on your own.\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician:** If you are finding it hard to stick with your plan, tell your care team. Structured diabetes self-management education is described as a core part of care, and it can help. A dietitian referral is also part of standard diabetes care. Your team can adjust the plan to fit your life.\n\nI could not find information in the retrieved material on exactly how much adherence changes HbA1c for an individual, so I cannot give a specific figure.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
